xt contains \"history of\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":false,"statement":"","confidence":0},"academicOrientation":{"score":55,"confidence":0.55},"profileConfidence":0.52,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Tahiti","confidence":0.38},{"name":"ancient Athens","confidence":0.37},{"name":"South Seas","confidence":0.36}],"argument":{"statement":"Medicine’s evolution is driven above all by changing cultural beliefs about understanding disease origins and controlling them, which in turn inspired major developments in medical science, practice, and public health systems.","confidence":0.46}},"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"3a5efbf443de3af3cdfcd71de48937e3f5770fae94f92163a261047d00052bdb","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Medicine & Public Health","secondarySubjects":["History"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily examines the evolution of medicine throughout history, focusing on medical practices, public health systems, and the broader cultural context in which they developed.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"8cbac6a155572d773fd60986aca9bfb3daf58650231b6dcc97496b6d22a03caf","summary":"\"Roy Porter explores medicine's evolution against the backdrop of the wider religious, scientific, philosophical, and political beliefs of the culture in which it develops, and he shows how our need to understand where diseases come from and what we can do to control them has - perhaps above all elseinspired developments in medicine through the ages. He charts the remarkable rise of modern medical science - the emergence of specialties such as anatomy, physiology, neurology, and bacteriology - as well as the accompanying development of wider medical practice at the bedside, in the hospital, and in the ambitious public health systems of the twentieth century. Along the way the book offers up a treasure trove of historical surprises: how the ancient Egyptians treated incipient baldness with a mixture of hippopotamus, lion, crocodile, goose, snake, and ibex fat; how a mystery epidemic devastated ancient Athens and brought an end to the domination of that great city: how lemons did as much as Nelson to defeat Napoleon: how yellow fever, carried by African mosquitoes to the Americas, led the French to fail utterly in their attempts to recover Haiti after the slave revolt of 1790: and how the explorers of the South Seas brought both syphilis to Tahiti and tuberculosis and measles to the Maoris.\"--Jacket.","displaySummary":"He charts the remarkable rise of modern medical science - the emergence of specialties such as anatomy, physiology, neurology, and bacteriology - as well as the accompanying development of wider medical practice at the bedside, in the hospital, and in the ambitious public health systems of the twentieth century. Along the way the book offers up a treasure trove of historical surprises: how the ancient Egyptians treated incipient baldness with a mixture of hippopotamus, lion, crocodile, goose, snake, and ibex fat; how a mystery epidemic devastated ancient Athens and brought an end to the domination of that great city: how lemons did as much as Nelson to defeat Napoleon: how yellow fever, carried by African mosquitoes to the Americas, led the French to fail utterly in their attempts to recover Haiti after the slave revolt of 1790: and how the explorers of the South Seas brought both syphilis to Tahiti and tuberculosis and measles to the Maoris.\"--Jacket.","primaryTopic":"Medicine, Health & the Body","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Medicine & Public Health","confidence":"high","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Medicine & Public Health","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"Medicine & Public Health","mappedSubject":"